{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"condition-variables","title":"Condition Variables & Producer–Consumer","group":"Concurrency","order":72,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <code>std::condition_variable</code> lets a thread sleep until another thread says “something changed”. Combined with a mutex and a queue it gives the classic producer–consumer pattern.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-push\"></i>A thread-safe queue</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;condition_variable&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;mutex&gt;\n#include &lt;optional&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nclass BlockingQueue {\n    std::queue&lt;T&gt; q_;\n    std::mutex m_;\n    std::condition_variable cv_;\n    bool closed_ = false;\npublic:\n    void push(T v) {\n        { std::lock_guard lock(m_); q_.push(std::move(v)); }\n        cv_.notify_one();                                     // wake one waiting consumer\n    }\n    void close() {\n        { std::lock_guard lock(m_); closed_ = true; }\n        cv_.notify_all();\n    }\n    std::optional&lt;T&gt; pop() {\n        std::unique_lock lock(m_);\n        cv_.wait(lock, [&amp;] { return !q_.empty() || closed_; });   // predicate guards spurious wake-ups\n        if (q_.empty()) return std::nullopt;                   // closed and drained\n        T v = std::move(q_.front());\n        q_.pop();\n        return v;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    BlockingQueue&lt;int&gt; jobs;\n    long long total = 0;\n    int handled = 0;\n    {\n        std::jthread consumer([&amp;] {\n            while (auto job = jobs.pop()) { total += *job; ++handled; }\n        });\n        std::jthread producer([&amp;] {\n            for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 100; ++i) jobs.push(i);\n            jobs.close();\n        });\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; handled &lt;&lt; \" jobs, total \" &lt;&lt; total &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>100 jobs, total 5050</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Lock</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The waiter takes a <code>std::unique_lock</code> on the mutex that protects the shared state.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Wait with a predicate</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>cv.wait(lock, pred)</code> checks <code>pred</code>; if false it <em>atomically</em> unlocks and sleeps, then relocks when woken and re-checks.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Modify, then notify</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The producer changes the state <strong>under the mutex</strong>, releases it, and calls <code>notify_one()</code> / <code>notify_all()</code>.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Always wait with a predicate (or in a <code>while</code> loop). Condition variables can wake up <em>spuriously</em>, and a notification sent before the waiter started waiting is lost — the predicate covers both cases.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does condition_variable::wait need a unique_lock rather than a lock_guard?</summary><div><p><code>wait</code> must unlock the mutex while sleeping and relock it before returning. <code>lock_guard</code> can't be unlocked and relocked; <code>unique_lock</code> can.</p></div></details></section>"}